hey guys this is just a quick improvised  tutorial on some tips and tricks in  planet coaster it's a little bit  unorganized but it was requested and I  promised and it might give some good  insight into what I'm doing in my  regular episodes except at 12 times the  speed so it might not be as easy to  catch so first in any building you built  you're limited to the grid which is the  size of a basic wall as you can see but  you can actually make the grid smaller  by placing a sandstone curved column and  just removing it directly afterwards and  now you'll just be able to place on  quarter tile grids likewise if you just  start building a building you're only be  able to raise it in full wall height  increments but if you very quickly place  one of these flat roofs and remove it  shortly afterwards  you'll actually be able to instantly  place everything in very small  increments like this you can also put  walls at different angles within one  building by actually exploiting the  duplication in the game for instance if  you place down one wall like this then  completely exit out of the building then  you click on the wall and press control  D to duplicate it then you'll be able to  place another one and freely rotate it's  completely independent from the other  wall which you placed and each time you  place it you'll be given a new one and  if you're done with this you just right  click and leave out of that and if you  click on this this is one building and  with this trick you can actually make  some nice curbs and fences for paths for  instance placing down a sandstone flat  roof edge exiting out of that using ctrl  D to duplicate it and you'll be able to  just place it alongside the path like  this actually get rid of rotations now  makes it a little bit easier and make  some custom curves these by the way are  some really good objects to use for  curbs and fences and if you want to go  the extra mile you can use the wooden  balconies and connect them to these  curves in such a way that you can make a  little wooden railing be sure to sink  them deep enough into the ground though  just like that using this you can also  put some matches on your path just  duplicate this wooden roof piece and put  it wherever you like these three  chimneys over here can be used for a lot  of things that aren't actually chimneys  lower them into a roof and line them up  with windows to make them look like  dormers use them as the bottoms or tops  of pillows or stack them to make some  Asian lanterns  or posts for fences and balustrade  now when making cross beams or details  and buildings it's really important to  have good control of the control and the  shift-key and basically what it means is  that shift is going to sink in objects  into anything which you hold your mouse  on and control is going to move you that  objects vertically or horizontally in  relation to the object on which you're  holding your mouse so take for instance  I would take this balcony if I point the  mouse towards this wall and you shift I  can sink it into the wall or bring it  out from it and if I press control I can  move it up or down or left or right now  the same goes for any other surface so  if it's an inclined surface it's going  to sink into that in an incline now you  can use this trick to very precisely  move objects so say for instance I'm on  this roof right now  I would sink it into the roof to bring  it right there and to move it here I  would have to use control but now my  mouse is pointed to what the wall so if  I press shift now I can move it into the  same direction as I just now did so  always keep in mind what your mouse is  pointing at and use ctrl + shift to your  advantage to move any object anywhere  the easiest way to make horizontal  crossbeams is to use the wood platform  overhang which we use for a bench  earlier and just sink it into a wall  just like that with the narrow thing  sticking out alternatively if you want  to have a thicker beam you just spin it  around and put it in there and you have  a very thick cross beam and if you want  to have a diagonal cross beam you take  the wood balcony put it on a roof like  this and bring it out to the front here  using control then using shift then  using control again you can sink it down  if your mouse is on the walls and by  using shift from this point onward you  can sink it into the wall  now since an object will rotate itself  based on the surface which are placing  it on you can actually place some mock  objects to get a specific rotation which  you need for instance if I want to make  a less steep cross beam you can place a  roof like this and then very similar to  how I just did it place a balcony on it  and sink it into this building bring it  out using shifts and maybe just slightly  change that placement like that and  there you go you can also use this to  make corner stones for your buildings  if you take the roof modeling stone  flats and put it on the opposite surface  of where you want to put the cornerstone  and then sink it into it and drag it  down you can actually make a row of  cornerstones one thing to keep in mind  when you're doing this though is to make  sure at all times to keep your cursor  pointed at the wall on which you  originally place the molding because if  you don't and you accidentally click on  the grass for instance your thing will  rotate and you'll have to place it all  over again similar to this you can also  make some straight concrete posts and  any building by placing it on the  opposite side sinking it into the wall  like this and then simply stack it this  is also a good example of how the  rotation of an object also determines on  the place within that object that you  may have your mouse over for instance if  I would click right now and place this  building while having my mouse over this  wall the thing would instantly rotate  and be useless but if I keep the cursor  exactly on that face of the object which  which faces the same direction as where  I originally place the object I can  still place it and it won't be doing  anything weird when it comes to  rotations Errol's make some pretty good  planters just sink them into a path just  like this until only a small Ridge is  left and then place whatever tree you  want to place on it I'd like to place a  Cypress on it and you have some small  planters this game doesn't offer any  ferns so far but you can actually sink a  palm into the ground a little bit and  make it look like quite a fern and this  is how it looks lowering the bushes  allows you to make some sweet flowerbeds  even with this yellow maple tree there  we go and the same goes for this one  really just make sure it's low enough  for the flowers to still be there and  there you go so yeah that's all for this  video I might make some actual series  and in that tutorials at some point in  the future but since it's just very  early for me as well and the game is  still really much in alpha I figured  this might be a bit more helpful for  people especially those who don't have a  lot of experience in the game yet or  room or we're wondering about a couple  of things in my time lapses  you
 

